<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kittur Chennamma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1778">1778</a>–<a href="/years/1829">1829</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Rani Chennamma</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kittur Chennamma was queen of Kittur, a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayatism">Lingayat</a> state in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> refused to recognise her adopted son under the doctrine of lapse, she led an armed revolt in 1824 and her forces killed the British collector at Kittur. Captured after a second campaign, she died in prison at Bailhongal and is honoured as an early resister of British rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kittur-chennamma.jpg" alt="Kittur Chennamma" width="320" height="254" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suma · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kittur_Chenamma.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
